From: Chris Bongaarts <cab@bongalow.net>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 01:05:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] 09765: Decompiler: allow wrapped memory ranges in processor
 spaces (fixes #4148)

Decompiler: allow wrapped memory ranges in processor spaces

GP-6936 added constructWrappingAddress() and the allows_wrapped_range
flag, but only the SpacebaseSpace constructors ever set the flag, so a
genuine wrapped access in an ordinary processor space (e.g. a 4-byte
read at 0xffffffff in ram) still throws "Trying to construct memory
range beyond end of address space".

Derive the flag in AddrSpace::calcScaleMask(), which every path that
sets a space's size goes through (both constructors, decode, and
truncateSpace): a full-size processor space is modular and wraps; a
truncated one is a logical window and does not.  Spacebase spaces are
untouched, so datatests/wraprange.xml is unaffected.

Adds datatests/wraprange2.xml (x86:LE:32 `mov eax,[0xffffffff]`),
which fails on master with the error above.

Fixes #4148.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CoB4STrwWeNM8AXG1z2wCn

include new decompiler test file in certification.manifest
---
 .../Decompiler/certification.manifest         |  1 +
 .../Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/space.cc     |  8 ++++++++
 .../src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml    | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 28 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml

diff --git a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/certification.manifest b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/certification.manifest
index d03f9d7ad8..e8296dfe5b 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/certification.manifest
+++ b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/certification.manifest
@@ -133,6 +133,7 @@ src/decompile/datatests/varcross.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/datatests/volatilemcs96dup.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/datatests/wayoffarray.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/datatests/wraprange.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
+src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/datatests/zeroload.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/datatests/zerotop.xml||GHIDRA||||END|
 src/decompile/zlib/README.txt||GHIDRA||||END|
diff --git a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/space.cc b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/space.cc
index 9dca8be3f6..fef2a3e5dd 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/space.cc
+++ b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/space.cc
@@ -42,6 +42,14 @@ void AddrSpace::calcScaleMask(void)
   uintb bufferSize = (addressSize < 3) ? 0x100 : 0x1000;
   pointerLowerBound += bufferSize;
   pointerUpperBound -= bufferSize;
+  if (type == IPTR_PROCESSOR) {
+    // A full-size processor space is modular, so an access running off its top end
+    // continues at its bottom.  A truncated space is only a logical window and does not.
+    if ((flags & truncated) == 0)
+      flags |= allows_wrapped_range;
+    else
+      flags &= ~allows_wrapped_range;
+  }
 }
 
 /// Initialize an address space with its basic attributes
diff --git a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..3643b1636b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/datatests/wraprange2.xml
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+<decompilertest>
+<!--
+    Example of a memory access that wraps from the top of the ram space to the bottom
+-->
+<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
+<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000" readonly="true">
+a1ffffffffc3
+</bytechunk>
+<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="wrapram"/>
+</binaryimage>
+<script>
+  <com>load function wrapram</com>
+  <com>decompile</com>
+  <com>print raw</com>
+  <com>quit</com>
+</script>
+<stringmatch name="Wrapped ram range #1" min="1" max="1">j\{r0x00000000,r0xffffffff\}.* = CONCAT31\(r0x00000000:3\(i\),r0xffffffff:1\(i\)\)</stringmatch>
+<stringmatch name="Wrapped ram range #2" min="1" max="1">EAX\(.*\) = j\{r0x00000000,r0xffffffff\}</stringmatch>
+</decompilertest>
